= Buckwheat

_Ayurvedic status: not classical — buckwheat is post-classical to South Asia (Himalayan cultivation, kuṭṭū / phāphar as a fasting-day food in late-medieval Hindu vrata practice). What follows under Ayurveda is reasoned analysis, not citation. Chinese: fully classical — 蕎麥 is a Bencao drug, with the bitter variant 苦蕎麥 as a distinct related drug. Greco-Roman: no tradition — buckwheat reached Europe only in the late medieval period from Central Asia, and the European medical tradition consists of the herb (Fagopyri herba) as a source of rutin._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Fagopyrum esculentum_ Moench, family Polygonaceae — *not a grass, not a true cereal* but a pseudocereal in the buckwheat family (sister to rhubarb, sorrel, knotweed). The drug-foodstuff is the achene (the triangular fruit, properly a single-seeded indehiscent dry fruit, not a true grain). The genus name is itself the etymological joke: _Fagopyrum_ = Latin _fagus_ ("beech") + Greek _pyrós_ ("wheat") = "beech-wheat," because the triangular achenes resemble beechnuts. _esculentum_ = "edible." The closely related Tatar / bitter buckwheat is _Fagopyrum tataricum_ (L.) Gaertn., a distinct species with higher rutin and a bitter principle.#footnote[Genus etymology: Lewis & Short, _A Latin Dictionary_ (s.v. _fagus_); Liddell & Scott, _A Greek–English Lexicon_ (s.v. _πυρός_); standard botanical sources for _Fagopyrum esculentum_ and _F. tataricum_ (e.g. _Flora of China_, vol. 5, Polygonaceae). The pseudo-cereal status (i.e. used as a grain but not a Poaceae) is botanical fact and is the basis of the gluten-free status discussed below.]

*English:* _Buckwheat_, a calque of Middle Dutch _boecweite_ or German _Buchweizen_, both = "beech-wheat" (the same metaphor as _Fagopyrum_) — from the resemblance of the triangular achenes to beechnuts. Old English _bōc_ = "beech." First English attestation 16th c., the word entering English with the crop itself.#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "buckwheat, n."); Kluge, _Etymologisches Wörterbuch der deutschen Sprache_ (s.v. _Buchweizen_); the Dutch/German calque history is standard.]

*Chinese:* 蕎麥 (qiáomài) — the canonical name. 蕎 (qiáo) here functions phonetically; 麥 (mài) is the wheat/barley graph, applied loosely to grain-like staples despite buckwheat not being a true _mài_. Distinct named forms:
- *蕎麥 / 甜蕎(麥) (qiáomài / tiánqiáo / tiánqiáomài)* — common (sweet) buckwheat, _F. esculentum_.
- *苦蕎(麥) (kǔqiáomài)* — Tatar / bitter buckwheat, _F. tataricum_; a distinct drug with stronger heat-clearing and stagnation-moving action, and much higher rutin content. Treated as a separate item.
- *蕎麥麵 (qiáomàimiàn)* — buckwheat noodles.
- *蕎麥粉 (qiáomàifěn)* — buckwheat flour.
- *蕎麥殼 (qiáomàiké)* — buckwheat hulls (used as pillow stuffing, etc.; minor folk uses).#footnote[Character glosses: _Hanyu Da Cidian_ (漢語大詞典); the 蕎麥 / 苦蕎麥 drug-form distinction follows the standard materia medica — see Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578, grain section, and Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (Eastland Press, 2004).]

*Sanskrit:* No classical entry. Late-medieval / modern Indic vocabulary: कुट्टू (_kuṭṭū_) in modern Hindi, फाफर (_phāphar_) and ओखला (_okhalā_) in regional Himalayan languages; the modern Sanskritic coinage _kuṭṭu-pīṣṭa_ ("buckwheat flour") is descriptive, not classical. Honest: no Sanskrit canonical name. *Cultural-religious note:* _kuṭṭū_ is the canonical _vrata_-day (fasting-day) flour in much of north Indian Hindu practice — eaten on Navarātri, Śivarātri, and ekādaśī fasts as a "non-grain" (not a true _anna_ / true cereal grain) and therefore permitted in the fast. This is a late-medieval/post-medieval religious-dietary tradition rooted in buckwheat's botanical non-grain status, not in Ayurvedic dravyaguna citation.#footnote[The non-existence of a classical Sanskrit name reflects buckwheat's late introduction to South Asia (Himalayan cultivation from Central Asia / Tibet, with downstream agricultural adoption). The _vrata_-food role: cultural-religious practice well attested in regional Hindu texts and ethnographic literature; it rests on the technical classification of buckwheat as a pseudocereal (not a Poaceae) — explicitly flagged as cultural-religious, not dravyaguna citation.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

蕎麥 is a fully canonical drug, entered in _Bencao Gangmu_ (and earlier sources) with both food-medicinal and topical applications; the bitter variant *苦蕎麥* is treated separately, with stronger heat-clearing and stagnation-moving actions.#footnote[蕎麥 entry: Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578, grain section; earlier attestation in the _Tujing Bencao_ (圖經本草, 1061) and other Song-period sources. 苦蕎麥 as a distinct related drug: also _Bencao Gangmu_; systematized in modern materia medica (Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.). The Chinese tradition treats 蕎麥 primarily as a dietary-medicinal grain with secondary topical uses, not as a complex-formula herb.]

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Cool to cold (涼 / 寒) — somewhat variant across sources; _Bencao Gangmu_ gives sweet/cool with a slightly dispersing quality; 苦蕎麥 is colder and more clearly bitter.

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Sweet (甘 gān), with a slight bitterness (微苦) for common buckwheat; distinctly bitter (苦) for 苦蕎麥.

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Large Intestine (大腸).#footnote[Property, flavor, channels: _Bencao Gangmu_ 蕎麥 entry and modern systematization in Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; variant attributions (涼 vs. 寒) and the differential bitter intensity between 甜蕎 and 苦蕎 are flagged.]

*Actions & indications:*
- 健脾消積 — strengthens Spleen and disperses food stagnation (the canonical action — buckwheat is the classical Chinese "moves stuck food" grain).
- 下氣寬腸 — downbears qi and opens the intestines; useful in postprandial fullness and food-stagnation constipation.
- 解毒斂瘡 — resolves toxicity, contracts/heals sores; topical paste of buckwheat flour was a classical application for skin sores, scalds, and chronic ulcers.
- 止瀉 — in some sources, helpful in chronic dysenteric diarrhea (the slightly astringent/bitter aspect).
- 止汗 — _Bencao Gangmu_ mentions buckwheat for "halting sweating" in some preparations (a minor application).
- *苦蕎麥 specifically:* stronger 清熱解毒 (heat-clearing), 化濕 (transforming damp), and modern uses for cardiovascular health (rutin-rich); used in tea form (苦蕎茶) — the bitter variant has migrated into modern Chinese functional-food / cardiovascular tradition.#footnote[Action list: _Bencao Gangmu_ 蕎麥 entry; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. The 苦蕎茶 / cardiovascular use is *modern* Chinese functional-food tradition with a pharmacological rutin basis — the modern use is grounded in classical heat-clearing but its specific cardiovascular framing is modern; flagged.]

*Cautions:* Cool/cold nature — unsuitable in Spleen-Stomach vacuity-cold with chronic diarrhea; for Vāta-equivalent dryness/cold constitutions, should be eaten with warming accompaniments. *Folk caution:* classical and folk sources warn against combining buckwheat with pork (豬肉) — the basis is humoral / sympathetic-medicine rather than toxicological, and is *not* supported by modern data. Heavy consumption of unwashed/poorly-prepared buckwheat in the flowering / fresh state has historically been associated (in farm-animal literature) with *fagopyrism* — see modern pharmacology section.#footnote[The 蕎麥忌豬肉 folk caution: _Bencao Gangmu_ and standard folk-pharmacy; flagged as folk (F) — no modern toxicological basis. Fagopyrism (photosensitization) is a real veterinary syndrome from fagopyrin in plant tops, distinct from grain consumption — discussed below.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_No classical Sanskrit entry. The placement below is reasoned analysis on cross-system grounds (the Chinese profile + the modern phytochemistry), and on the Indic vernacular tradition of_ kuṭṭū / phāphar _as a vrata-food. Flagged as interpretive throughout._

*Reasoned rasa:* Madhura (मधुर, sweet) — the principal taste; Kaṣāya (कषाय, astringent) — secondary; Tikta (तिक्त, bitter) — distinctly present in bitter buckwheat (_F. tataricum_), faint in common buckwheat.

*Reasoned guṇa:* Laghu (लघु, light), Rūkṣa (रूक्ष, dry) — well supported by the high fiber, modest fat, and the rapid cooking / non-clinging cooked texture; the _vrata_-food role rests on these qualities (light and dry, suitable for a fast).

*Reasoned vīrya:* Śīta (शीत, cooling) — consistent with the Chinese 涼/寒 attribution.

*Reasoned vipāka:* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent) — by analogy with other dry-light pseudocereals; some interpretations give Madhura.#footnote[All of rasa-guṇa-vīrya-vipāka here is reasoned cross-system extension, anchored on the Chinese 蕎麥 entry, the Indian _vrata_-food role, and modern phytochemistry. There is no classical Sanskrit citation. P. V. Sharma's _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_ (vol. II) discusses _kuṭṭu_ in this reasoned register; this is the most authoritative modern Indian dravyaguna placement and is itself interpretive. Flagged accordingly.]

*Prabhāva (specific action):* No classical prabhāva (because no classical entry). On a reasoned basis: the *high rutin content* — capillary-strengthening, an action not derivable from sweet-astringent-light-dry-cooling-pungent profile alone — could be argued at the prabhāva level if one were to formalize a modern Ayurvedic placement; this is exactly the cross-system case where modern phytochemistry surfaces an action the saṃhitās would have had to name at the prabhāva level had the substance been canonical. Explicitly interpretive, not stated in any classical text.

*Reasoned doṣa effect:* Pacifies Pitta and Kapha; aggravates Vāta if eaten without unctuous preparation. Suits the _vrata_-fast register precisely because the lightness and dryness, when prepared with a touch of ghee, do not overload digestion during ritual fasting.

*Reasoned karma:* _Laghu-pathya_ (light and wholesome — for the fasting day), _grāhī_ (binding stools), _ruci-vardhana_ (in proper preparation), _vraṇa-ropaṇa_ (external, by parallel with the Chinese topical use), _hṛd-rakṣaka_ (cardiac-supportive — modern, via rutin). The buckwheat-flour _puri_ / _roti_ / _halwā_ of vrata cooking embodies these by traditional preparation.#footnote[The vrata-cuisine technical placement (Vāta-corrected with ghee, eaten with potato/sago and curd or rock salt) is the traditional cooking knowledge that supplies the appropriate _saṃskāra_. This is folk-religious dietetic tradition; the Ayurvedic karma placement is interpretive.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk/regional, M = modern/pharmacological)_

No classical Ayurvedic viruddha for buckwheat — it is not in the saṃhitās.

- *(F) Chinese folk:* 蕎麥 + pork (豬肉) — classical caution in some _Bencao_ folk lore; no modern toxicological basis.
- *(F) Indic regional:* the vrata cuisine has internal stylistic rules (use of rock salt _sendhā namak_ instead of refined salt, exclusion of certain spices) — these are *religious-ritual* rules, not dravyaguna viruddha; should not be confused with the latter.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Buckwheat allergy (IgE-mediated):* a serious and not-uncommon allergen, particularly in regions where buckwheat is a staple (Japan, Korea, parts of China, parts of Europe); can cause anaphylaxis. Cross-reactivity with other Polygonaceae and with latex documented in cases. The most clinically important point in the section.
  - *Fagopyrism (photosensitization):* a documented syndrome in livestock (and rarely in humans) from heavy ingestion of the *flowering plant tops*, due to the photodynamic naphthodianthrone *fagopyrin* (related to hypericin from St John's wort). The *seed/grain* is essentially fagopyrin-free and safe; fagopyrism is a concern with the plant rather than the food.
  - *Anticoagulant interaction (theoretical):* very high rutin / Tatar-buckwheat intake — especially in supplement form — could theoretically interact with anticoagulants; not well documented clinically.
  - *Other:* heavy contamination of buckwheat with the toxic seed of _Datura_ (jimsonweed) has been documented in the agricultural literature as a real (rare) contamination risk; sourcing matters.#footnote[Buckwheat IgE allergy: well documented in allergology literature, especially Japanese and Korean (verify against current reviews in allergy journals). Fagopyrism: described in veterinary medicine (chiefly sheep and cattle) and rare human cases; the molecular agent is fagopyrin — see phytochemistry literature on _Hypericum_-like naphthodianthrones. The Datura contamination risk: noted in agricultural food-safety literature; sourcing matters.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

Buckwheat is *not classical in Tibb*. The species is a Central-Asian / East-Asian pseudocereal that reached Europe in the late medieval period (14th–15th c.) by way of the Mongol and Russian transmission routes — postdating the medieval Greco-Arabic medical tradition; it does not appear in Avicenna or Ibn al-Bayṭār. Modern Arabic / Persian / Urdu terms are post-classical descriptive coinages: *ḥinṭa al-qarāqush* (حنطة القراقش) and *siyāh dāne* (سیاہ دانہ, "black seed" — different from the Prophetic-medicine _ḥabba al-sawdā_ / _Nigella sativa_, which is also called *siyāh dāne* — a real naming confusion). Modern Unani usage in South Asia treats buckwheat as a vrata fasting-food (the Hindu register; not Tibb-canonical) and as a gluten-free grain for celiac patients (modern adoption). No substantive medieval Tibb pharmacology.

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

蕎麥 figures more as a dietetic-medicinal grain than as a multi-herb formula ingredient.

- *蕎麥粥 (Qiáomài Zhōu)* — buckwheat congee, for food-stagnation, postprandial fullness, and chronic constipation; the canonical dietetic preparation.
- *蕎麥麵 (Qiáomàimiàn)* — buckwheat noodles; eaten cold in summer in northern China (and similarly Japanese _soba_), where the cool-clearing-stagnation-moving action is dietetically appropriate. (The Japanese soba tradition is a major culinary descent of the Chinese 蕎麥麵 family.)
- *Topical buckwheat-flour paste* — for skin sores, scalds, mild burns, and prickly heat; mixed with water or vinegar; classical and continuing folk practice. The 解毒斂瘡 action operationalized.
- *苦蕎茶 (Kǔqiáo Chá)* — Tatar-buckwheat tea, a parched-grain infusion used as a modern cardiovascular and metabolic functional food; the rutin-rich application — flagged as *modern* (not classical) although rooted in the classical Bitter-buckwheat heat-clearing principle.
- *蕎麥殼 pillow filling* — buckwheat hulls used to stuff therapeutic pillows (folk: for headache, insomnia, neck tension); cultural rather than formula-pharmaceutical.#footnote[Dietetic/topical applications: _Bencao Gangmu_ 蕎麥 entry; standard Chinese dietetics (e.g. Kastner, _Chinese Nutrition Therapy_). The 苦蕎茶 cardiovascular framing is modern functional-food usage; flagged.]

=== Ayurveda

No classical Ayurvedic formulas use buckwheat. The Indic culinary tradition for buckwheat is the *vrata* (Hindu fasting-day) cuisine, an unbroken folk-religious tradition primarily in north India:

- *Kuṭṭū kī puri (कुट्टू की पूरी)* — buckwheat-flour fried bread, eaten on Navarātri and ekādaśī fasts.
- *Kuṭṭū kā parathā / chillā* — flatbreads / pancakes.
- *Kuṭṭū kā halwā* — sweet roasted-flour dish.
- *Phāphar / kuṭṭū roti* in Uttarakhand and Himachal — Himalayan everyday consumption as a roti staple, not vrata-specific.
- *Vrata-cuisine technicalities:* paired with potato, sago (sabudana), and curd, salted with rock salt (sendhā namak), and seasoned only with the spices permitted in the fast (cumin, green chilli, ginger, ghee).#footnote[The vrata-cuisine repertoire is described in regional Indian cookery and ethnographic literature; the religious-dietary framework underlying it (the prohibition of true _anna_ during _vrata_ and the consequent use of pseudocereals — buckwheat, amaranth, water-chestnut flour _siṅghāḍā_ — and ṣaṣṭika-class fast-permitted items) is well attested. None of this is in the Ayurvedic saṃhitās; the framing is religious-dietary, flagged as such.]

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

No Greco-Roman pharmacy of buckwheat — the crop reached western and central Europe only in the late medieval period (chiefly 14th–15th c.) by way of Central-Asian and Russian transmission. The European medical tradition is correspondingly late but has a coherent pharmacological strand:

- *Late-medieval / early modern European cuisine:* established as a staple in Brittany (galettes / _galettes de blé noir_, "black-wheat galettes"), in the Netherlands and Germany (_Buchweizengrütze_, _pannekoeken_), in Russia and Ukraine (_kasha_, an iconic Slavic dish), in Poland, and in Italian Alpine cooking (Lombard _pizzoccheri_). The reach of buckwheat in European peasant cuisine was wide.
- *Herbalist tradition:* European herbalists from the 16th c. onward note buckwheat tea / flour as a mild astringent and demulcent, with secondary fame as a bee-plant (an excellent late-summer nectar source, hence the dark, distinctive _miel de sarrasin_ / buckwheat honey).
- *Modern pharmacy — Fagopyri herba and rutin:* the *flowering tops* of common buckwheat (_Fagopyri herba_) are an official drug in several European pharmacopoeias as a source of rutin, the flavonol-glycoside whose vasoprotective use in capillary fragility, chronic venous insufficiency, and (historically) radiation-induced microvascular damage gave rise to the *rutoside* and *troxerutin* class of phlebotonic semi-synthetic drugs. This is the genuine modern European pharmacy of buckwheat — distinct from the grain.
- *Modern celiac-relevant use:* as a gluten-free pseudocereal, buckwheat is a major staple in modern celiac-safe diets.#footnote[Late-medieval European introduction: standard agronomic history (e.g. Watson, _Agricultural Innovation in the Early Islamic World_, and other agricultural histories). Brittany galette, kasha, etc.: regional cuisine and food-history literature. _Fagopyri herba_ as official drug: ESCOP and various European pharmacopoeias (Czech, German Commission E historical monographs) — verify current monograph status. Rutin / troxerutin clinical pharmacology: phlebotonic clinical literature — verify current systematic reviews. The modern celiac-safe role: standard nutritional dietetics for gluten-free diets.]

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Composition, dehulled buckwheat groats (raw), approximate values per 100 g — *verify against USDA FoodData Central* (current edition). Tatar / bitter buckwheat (_F. tataricum_) has substantially higher rutin (often 1–6% by dry weight, vs. ~0.01–0.1% in common buckwheat) and a more bitter flavor.#footnote[Composition figures are approximate; verify against USDA FoodData Central (https://fdc.nal.usda.gov), current edition. Rutin content differential between _F. esculentum_ and _F. tataricum_: well documented; current values in Tatar buckwheat are often 50–100× those of common buckwheat — verify against current phytochemistry literature.]

- Energy ~340 kcal; Water ~10 g
- Protein ~13 g — *with a notably complete amino-acid profile* (unusual among grains/pseudocereals: adequate lysine, modest but not deficient methionine; biological value approaching legume-grain mixtures). Buckwheat is among the most nutritionally complete pseudocereals.
- Fat ~3.4 g
- Carbohydrate ~72 g (chiefly starch); Dietary fiber ~10 g
- *Gluten-free* — buckwheat is a Polygonaceae, not a Poaceae, and contains no gluten / no celiac-relevant epitopes. The single most important *modern* fact about buckwheat for the celiac and gluten-sensitive patient.
- Magnesium ~230 mg, manganese ~1.3 mg, copper, phosphorus, niacin, riboflavin — broadly favorable mineral profile
- *Rutin and quercetin* — flavonoid content; rutin is the signature compound, especially high in Tatar buckwheat
- *Fagopyrin* — present in flowering plant tops and minor seed traces; relevant to fagopyrism (see below)
- *D-chiro-inositol* — present and modestly studied for insulin-sensitization effects

*Pharmacology / physiology of note:*
- *Gluten-free pseudocereal:* the central modern dietetic fact. Buckwheat is one of the principal grain-substitutes in celiac and non-celiac gluten-sensitivity diets and gives a near-complete protein profile when paired with legumes or animal protein.
- *Rutin / vasoprotection:* the buckwheat-herb pharmacology — rutin and related flavonoids strengthen capillary integrity and reduce permeability; the phlebotonic class (rutosides, troxerutin) descends from this. Clinical evidence for chronic venous insufficiency is modest but real; for capillary-fragility states, well established.
- *Tatar (bitter) buckwheat:* high rutin, often a functional-food / tea preparation; cardiovascular and metabolic claims modestly supported.
- *Glycemic index:* buckwheat is *lower-GI* than wheat or rice, with a substantial soluble-fiber and resistant-starch contribution; useful in type-2 diabetes and metabolic syndrome.
- *D-chiro-inositol:* modest evidence for insulin sensitization; some interest in PCOS contexts.
- *Fagopyrism (photosensitization):* a real syndrome from heavy ingestion of *plant tops* (in livestock; rare in humans). Caused by fagopyrin, a naphthodianthrone related to hypericin from St John's wort. The grain itself contains negligible fagopyrin; this is a concern for the herb / unprocessed flowering plant, *not* the food.
- *Allergy:* see Viruddha (M) — serious IgE allergy possible, especially East Asian populations. The most clinically significant safety issue in normal grain use.
- *Cross-system convergence:* the Chinese 健脾消積 / 下氣寬腸 (Spleen-strengthening, food-stagnation-clearing, intestine-opening) action and the modern fiber / resistant-starch profile align cleanly; the modern rutin / vasoprotection action surfaces what would have to be called a prabhāva-level action had buckwheat been an Ayurvedic drug.#footnote[Gluten-free pseudocereal status: standard celiac dietetics (e.g. _American Journal of Gastroenterology_ celiac guidelines). Rutin / troxerutin clinical literature: phlebotonic-drug reviews. Tatar buckwheat / metabolic claims: preliminary clinical literature — verify against current systematic reviews. Fagopyrism: veterinary toxicology literature; the chemical agent fagopyrin and its parallel to hypericin are well characterized. Allergy: as above.]

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:* No toxicological upper limit for the grain — buckwheat is a whole food. Practical considerations:
- *As food:* the routine serving (~50–80 g raw / ~150–200 g cooked groats per meal) raises no specific safety question; buckwheat is appropriate for daily / staple consumption in the absence of allergy.
- *Tatar buckwheat tea (functional use):* commonly ~5–10 g of parched grain per cup, 1–3 cups/day; well tolerated short-term in normotensive adults; long-term high-rutin intake should be considered if anticoagulant therapy is co-administered.
- *Fagopyri herba (as a medicinal herb):* European-pharmacopoeial doses are typically of standardized rutoside / troxerutin, not the crude herb; for the crude herb, follow specific pharmacopoeial / herbalist guidance.
- *Risk groups (use cautiously or avoid):* (a) known *buckwheat allergy* — absolute avoidance; (b) anticoagulant therapy (case-by-case with high-rutin sources); (c) celiac patient — *favors* buckwheat (gluten-free), the only direction where the safety signal points the other way.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[These are reference thresholds; verify clinical figures (rutin / troxerutin / Fagopyri herba dosing) against current European pharmacopoeia and clinical-pharmacology literature; the picture is active in modern phytopharmacy. The "celiac patient — favors buckwheat" point is a deliberate exception note — for celiac patients buckwheat is one of the few staples that is *safer* than wheat. Clinical advice should be individualized.]

